    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Aksum, in highland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>, grew rich trading African <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and gold through its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adulis">Adulis</a>.</li><li>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ezana">King Ezana</a> it became one of the first states to adopt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, founding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> tradition.</li><li>Cut off from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> trade by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-muslim-conquests">Islamic conquests</a>, it shrank into a regional highland kingdom.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Aksum was one of the very few ancient states to mint its own gold coins, and the first to place the Christian cross on its coinage.</p></aside>
    </section>

